1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:03,370
Heat and flooding shape cold storage decisions.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:08,380
Before choosing a building, bring the records together and decide what to inspect.

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,060
This is GEONOS.

4
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:16,300
It connects a citywide map, public evidence and six Meta model roles in one investigation.

5
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:23,370
The NYC workspace, evidence pipeline and model integrations were built during the hackathon, using existing

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:26,380
public datasets and pretrained models.

7
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:31,340
Let’s follow two Smith Street buildings from their records to a practical site-visit plan.

8
00:00:33,405 --> 00:00:35,455
Start with the whole city.

9
00:00:35,485 --> 00:00:40,225
More than a million valid building outlines cover all five boroughs.

10
00:00:40,365 --> 00:00:44,305
At this scale, density makes the pattern readable.

11
00:00:44,605 --> 00:00:48,465
Zoom closer and individual outlines appear.

12
00:00:48,765 --> 00:00:54,225
Focus a borough, choose a contextual layer and read its legend.

13
00:00:54,765 --> 00:00:59,025
Switch to 3D to explore recorded roof heights.

14
00:01:01,113 --> 00:01:06,443
Search an address, building identification number or tax-lot number.

15
00:01:06,473 --> 00:01:09,933
The result identifies which record you’re opening.

16
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:17,133
A tax lot can link to several buildings, so those identifiers help you follow the relationship.

17
00:01:17,193 --> 00:01:23,933
Use filters to narrow the records, or Search this view to work with the area on screen.

18
00:01:24,153 --> 00:01:32,123
Move through the result pages, select a candidate, and keep your chosen places available as you continue

19
00:01:32,153 --> 00:01:33,453
exploring.

20
00:01:35,447 --> 00:01:38,857
Select one building to open its details on the left.

21
00:01:38,887 --> 00:01:43,737
Select a second and the panel becomes a comparison, with A and B on the map.

22
00:01:43,767 --> 00:01:48,057
Here, we have 572 and 592 Smith Street.

23
00:01:48,087 --> 00:01:54,617
Compare their mapped footprints at the same physical scale, then inspect recorded roof height, construction

24
00:01:54,647 --> 00:01:56,187
year and zoning.

25
00:01:56,247 --> 00:01:59,497
Whole-tax-lot floor area has its own label.

26
00:01:59,527 --> 00:02:03,707
Open the source references to follow each measurement back to its record.

27
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:08,550
Choose the measurement that matters to your project.

28
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:13,320
For this demonstration, set a condition of twenty thousand square feet.

29
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:19,960
You can investigate usable space, then check the condition against mapped footprint area.

30
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:25,720
The comparison shows each result and identifies a useful next fact to obtain.

31
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:33,160
Add project requirements, follow their evidence and prepare questions about electrical capacity.

32
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:38,840
An owner report can carry its source and date alongside the public records.

33
00:02:40,905 --> 00:02:46,285
Open the stormwater story to explore modeled surface water around the two buildings.

34
00:02:46,345 --> 00:02:52,605
Start with the reference points, move to the surrounding ground, then change the sea-level scenario.

35
00:02:52,825 --> 00:03:00,635
In the published 2050 view, the deep-water map class reaches 572’s outline and lies about forty-seven meters

36
00:03:00,665 --> 00:03:02,525
from 592.

37
00:03:02,985 --> 00:03:05,245
Both views share the same extent.

38
00:03:05,305 --> 00:03:11,275
Adjust the investigation band, inspect the scenario assumptions and read the source dates.

39
00:03:11,305 --> 00:03:17,725
Use that geographic context to plan entrance, drainage and equipment-elevation checks.

40
00:03:19,822 --> 00:03:22,832
Climate history adds the station perspective.

41
00:03:22,862 --> 00:03:29,202
Explore NOAA observations by year, month and metric, with period labels beside the charts.

42
00:03:29,422 --> 00:03:35,472
Then move to the regional future view, where published NPCC projections show their own time windows and

43
00:03:35,502 --> 00:03:36,592
baseline.

44
00:03:36,622 --> 00:03:43,072
Together, these views help you frame the operating horizon: what indoor conditions will the project need,

45
00:03:43,102 --> 00:03:49,442
which equipment questions deserve attention, and what should the team investigate for the period ahead?

46
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:57,310
The paired-weather view keeps air temperature and dew point from the same observation together.

47
00:03:57,370 --> 00:04:04,700
At JFK, explore the hottest-air and highest-dew-point samples, with their dates and accompanying readings.

48
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:09,630
Move through the observed hot period to see heat and moisture change together.

49
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:14,110
The chart also provides source coverage and quality information.

50
00:04:14,490 --> 00:04:20,940
Bring these paired observations into conversations about operating conditions, equipment performance and the

51
00:04:20,970 --> 00:04:23,230
measurements to collect at the building.

52
00:04:25,322 --> 00:04:29,372
For energy, follow a separate example at 211 Lombardy Street.

53
00:04:29,402 --> 00:04:33,852
Open the parent report, trace its linked buildings and inspect the child reports.

54
00:04:33,882 --> 00:04:39,692
Compare the reported floor areas and follow how each area enters the energy-intensity calculation.

55
00:04:39,722 --> 00:04:45,372
Prepare a request for the meter-to-building schedule, then ask the owner which meters, spaces and operating

56
00:04:45,402 --> 00:04:47,422
uses each report covers.

57
00:04:49,488 --> 00:04:53,108
Muse Voice turns a spoken question into editable text.

58
00:04:53,168 --> 00:04:57,058
Review the transcript, add it to your message and send when you’re ready.

59
00:04:57,088 --> 00:05:03,618
Muse Spark carries the conversation into the investigation, choosing tools for official records and evidence.

60
00:05:03,648 --> 00:05:08,738
Expand Sources to inspect the retrieved information and follow the tool trace.

61
00:05:08,768 --> 00:05:14,498
The chat stays alongside the map, so you can keep the selected buildings in view while refining the

62
00:05:14,528 --> 00:05:15,908
next question.

63
00:05:17,988 --> 00:05:25,318
For the visual comparison, GEONOS retrieves official Spring 2024 aerial crops for both buildings.

64
00:05:25,348 --> 00:05:30,758
SAM 3.1 locates roof and tree image regions in those same crops.

65
00:05:30,788 --> 00:05:36,488
Switch between A and B, inspect the overlays, or enlarge the original image.

66
00:05:36,548 --> 00:05:40,648
Compare with map relates the image regions to the mapped footprint.

67
00:05:41,028 --> 00:05:45,288
The outside band adds approximate tree-mask context.

68
00:05:45,348 --> 00:05:52,488
These views give the project team specific places to examine during a roof, vegetation and equipment survey.

69
00:05:54,572 --> 00:06:00,462
Muse Wonder reviews structured summaries from both completed image analyses alongside the public records.

70
00:06:00,492 --> 00:06:06,782
Open its review to follow three lines of inquiry: roofs and equipment, tree context, and the relationship

71
00:06:06,812 --> 00:06:09,022
between the dated map and image.

72
00:06:09,052 --> 00:06:11,022
Each leads to a suggested check.

73
00:06:11,052 --> 00:06:14,862
You can also request a second review of the public-data comparison.

74
00:06:14,892 --> 00:06:20,672
The aim is a clear question for the owner, surveyor or project team to investigate next.

75
00:06:22,655 --> 00:06:32,915
Explore the site through time with official 2020 and 2024 imagery at matching geographic bounds.

76
00:06:34,975 --> 00:06:42,595
Run DINOv2 locally in your browser to highlight imagery windows for closer inspection.

77
00:06:42,655 --> 00:06:48,595
Its feature comparison produces a sixteen-by-sixteen grid.

78
00:06:49,215 --> 00:06:57,795
Move the divider between dates, inspect the original photographs and toggle the grid as you look around.

79
00:06:58,255 --> 00:07:06,515
Then prepare a site-visit question based on the areas you want to examine more closely.

80
00:07:08,530 --> 00:07:11,860
Muse Image illustrates the inspection plan.

81
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:19,220
A request turns the written checks into a conceptual diagram: what we know, what to verify and what

82
00:07:19,250 --> 00:07:20,420
could change.

83
00:07:20,450 --> 00:07:24,260
The displayed example carries its date and site references.

84
00:07:24,290 --> 00:07:29,270
Expand the diagram to discuss the sequence with an owner or project team.

85
00:07:29,490 --> 00:07:35,300
The written brief keeps its source links, giving the conversation a path from public evidence to the next

86
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:36,710
investigation.

87
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:43,760
Export the selected records, comparison and project conditions.

88
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:48,400
Open the methods and visual guide to revisit the sources behind them.

89
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:57,070
You now have a focused set of requests: roof plans, meter schedules, usable-space information and

90
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:59,600
electrical-capacity records.

91
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,030
Take those questions to the owner.

92
00:08:02,060 --> 00:08:07,790
On the site visit, inspect entrances, drainage and equipment elevations.

93
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:15,440
GEONOS helps turn a pair of candidate buildings into an organized evidence trail and a practical next action.
